# VR-Nexus frontend environment
#
# Copy to .env.local if you need to override anything. You do not need to for
# normal development — every value here has a working default, and the app runs
# with no .env file at all.
#
# Only variables prefixed VITE_ are exposed to the browser. That prefix is the
# whole security boundary, so never put a secret in this file or any .env file
# that Vite loads: everything VITE_ is inlined into the JavaScript bundle and is
# readable by anyone who opens devtools.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_API_BASE_URL — where the API lives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave it EMPTY in development. This is not laziness; it is the only mode that
# currently works in a browser.
#
# Empty means every request stays a relative path (/api/auth/login), so the
# browser only ever talks to the dev server on :5173, which forwards /api and
# /ws to 127.0.0.1:8000. Same origin, so the browser never sends a preflight and
# never applies CORS rules.
#
# Setting it to an absolute URL makes every request cross-origin instead:
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
#
# The backend's app/main.py does not register CORSMiddleware, so the browser
# blocks all of those requests before the server ever sees them. The API works
# perfectly in curl and Postman while the app shows nothing but network errors —
# an expensive afternoon if you don't know to look for it. The project's API
# handoff note asks for exactly this value, which is why it is spelled out here.
#
# If you do need the absolute URL — a deployed build, or a frontend served from
# a different host than the API — the backend has to allow the origin first:
#
#   from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware
#
#   app.add_middleware(
#       CORSMiddleware,
#       allow_origins=["http://localhost:5173"],
#       allow_credentials=True,
#       allow_methods=["*"],
#       allow_headers=["*"],
#   )
#
# allow_origins must list exact origins including the scheme and port.
# "*" is not usable here, because it is invalid in combination with
# allow_credentials=True and the browser rejects the pair.
#
# WebSocket URLs follow the same switch: with this empty they resolve against
# the page's own origin and pick ws:// or wss:// to match, so an https
# deployment does not silently attempt an insecure socket.
#
VITE_API_BASE_URL=
